3 STAE Västmanland
3 CTRY Sverige
1 BURI
2 DATE 18 JUN 1786
1 OCCU Doktor i teologi, präst
1 TITL Rev.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} †† labored for nine years in Pennsylvania and New Jersey. See [https://www.geni.com/projects/Journals-of-Rev-Henry-Melchior-M%C3%BChlenberg-Translated/4486230 <journals project>] . alphabetical list. ''The church i
2 CONC n which Dr. Wrangel labored is “Gloria Dei Church,” or the “Old Swedes Church,” on Swanson and Christian streets, Philadelphia.''
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Son of a Swedish Military Family==
2 CONT
2 CONT *https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Wrangel_af_Sage_och_Waschel_nr_1850
2 CONT
2 CONT "The family of Wrangel von Sage und Waschel moved from Germany to Estonia with the German Order. Carl Magnus' father, the army major [https://www.geni.com/people/Otto-Wilhelm-Wrangel-af-Sage-och-Waschel/6000000019855548595 Otto Wi
2 CONC lhelm Wrangel], was born in Reval (Tallinn, Estonia) in 1688, and moved to Sweden in 1709. Most of the men in the family were high-ranking military men. Under King Karl XII's various military campaigns at least 70 Wrangel men were
2 CONC  killed, of which 22 are buried at the Ukranian town of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Poltava Poltava] after the Russian defeat of the Swedish army. Several of the Wrangel family became leading politicians and governmen
2 CONC t administrators in Sweden. Carl Magnus was born into this famous family of nobility on August 23, 1727, in Moklinta Parish near the town of Sala in central Sweden. He was the only male member of the family who did not become a so
2 CONC ldier. On his mother's side, on the other hand, were several famous scientists and theologians. (10) As a result of the prolonged wars, great hunger and misery plagued the country, and after his father's death he and his family be
2 CONC came quite destitute and dependent upon the support of others in the family. Carl Magnus came under the ward of his mother's brother who sent him on a study trip to Strasbourg. At an age of 22 (1749) he came to Uppsala and became 
2 CONC a student under the famous professor Ihre, an historian, a linguistic genius and an outspoken political liberal. About two years later Wrangel switched from his political studies to the study of theology. Political life in Sweden 
2 CONC at that time was divided into two groups, [https://www.geni.com/projects/Hats-Russian-War-1741-1743/4475222 "hattarna" (the "hats " i.e., the nobility)] who had the ruling power, and "mossorna" (the "caps") who consisted largely o
2 CONC f craftsmen, shopkeepers, and farmers. Most of the professors belonged to the latter group. This was also a time of almost unlimited flattery, with copious and servile use of extremely flowery language and superlatives in addressi
2 CONC ng persons of rank and influence. Having the favor of such persons seemed to be necessary to gain any kind of position. Wrangel held a speech in which he symbolically lifted the six-year-old prince almost into heaven, praising God
2 CONC  for this greatest gift ever given to the Swedish people. Nevertheless, the prince was intelligent and a good student in his own right. The address created qutie an interest, and probably did not hurt Wrangel's chances to his late
2 CONC r being appoitned to the attractive position of chaplain to the court and spiritual counselor to the King. A year later (1752) he served as respondent at a disputation. His arguments were brilliant, and further gained the favor of
2 CONC  the royal couple. (11)
2 CONT
2 CONT The young, intelligent and industrious nobleman soon caught the interest of Archbishop Dr. Henrik Benzelius, who taught him personally, and strengthened his resolve to serve God and the church. The King, who was well acquainted wi
2 CONC th Wrangel's gifts, hard work and progress, recommended him to receive a scholarship at the Uppsala Academy, Wrangel himself having no funds for further studies. He began preaching in various churches. Even before ordained a pries
2 CONC t, he was invited in 1755 to preach in Ulriksdal Castle before the whole royal family. In an open letter dated May 2, 1756, the King called him to serve as chaplain to the Royal Court. At the same time he was also encouraged by th
2 CONC e King to go to Germany for further theologal studies. This opened for a temptation to adjust to the wishes and thoughts of the court. (12)
2 CONT
2 CONT At that time parts of Germany (Pommern) were ruled by Sweden. The University of Greifswald was located in that area, and was for all practical purposes, a Swedish-German institution. During the summer of 1756 the school celebrated
2 CONC  its founding 300 years earlier. Upon recommendation from the Faculty of Philosophy at Uppsala University, Wrangel was then appointed "Jubelmagister" ("Jubilee Master") in philosophy. From Greifswald he proceeded to Gottingen wher
2 CONC e he was enrolled October 26, 1756. After a year's studies, and after a solemn disputation act, the degree of Doctor of Theology was solemnly conferred upon him September 30, 1757. At the disputation Wrangel defended a dissertatio
2 CONC n on the Temptations of Jesus in the Desert. (13)
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon his return to Sweden, Wrangel was ordained by bishop Erik Alstrin in Strangnas Cathedral on March 17, 1758. (14) Shortly after (April 3,
2 CONT
2 CONT 1758) he was favored with The Royal Majesty's High Authorization to be their Ordinary Chaplain and Privy-Councillor, with seat and vote in the Consistory of the Royal Court, although without the enjoyment of any remuneration. (15)
2 CONC  This appointment did not occur without dissension and discussion. Some of those who should approve Wrangel for membership in the Consistory evidently did not favor German doctoral degrees. Some demanded new examinations to make s
2 CONC ure that he was qualified. But after a period of discussion he was finally 
2 CONT accepted. The Consistory handled matters concerning the behavior and performance of the clergy. At that time complaints were raised against several of the priests, alleging that they had broken their oath and the law in preaching 
2 CONC against the present form of government.
2 CONT
2 CONT The next month Wrangel's career took an unexpected turn. He had long been a sort of protege of Archbishop H, Benzelius, Benzelius was one of the most eager supporters of the Swedish mission on the Delaware in America, a mission wh
2 CONC ich went back to the failed Swedish colony of New Sweden. Wrangel wrote bishop Benzelius and also enclosed some papers. The Archbishop answered in a friendly letter, congratulating him on his high appointments, at the same time of
2 CONC fering him the position of '''''Provost (superintendent) for the Swedish churches in the Lower Delaware/Philadelphia area.''''' †† This was, of course, a great and difficult assignment. Wrangel thanked Benzelius for his confidence
2 CONC  in him, but felt he needed to find God's plan for his life. (16) Besides, he had an attack of a disease of the chest. But his final answer was affirmative. Unfortunately, the Archbishop died only twelve days later. As a result a 
2 CONC Church Board took over the administration of the work in America. A Rev. Acrelius, a former provost for that work, argued quite strongly that a priest who had just been ordained should not be assigned responsibility for such a dem
2 CONC anding and difficult work. Others were envious of Wrangel's rapid advancement and this new appointment, in addition to his having received the important position of Chaplain at the Royal Court. But he was eventually affirmed and r
2 CONC eceived his authorizations from the King June 12, 1758." ''~• from attached source''
2 CONT
2 CONT Sala stadsförsamlings kyrkoarkiv, Död- och begravningsböcker, SE/ULA/11305/F/1 (1775-1807), bildid: C0020211_00073
