2 TITL Lutheran ministers in early Pennsylvania & a few associated leaders
2 NOTE
3 CONT Were Lutherans and German Reformed buried together?
3 CONT
3 CONT AI Overview
3 CONT "Yes, German Lutherans and German Reformed have been buried together in cemeteries in multiple locations, including Pennsylvania, Kentucky, and Wisconsin." We read in <journals> that the Rev. HMM frequently says "buried in the Ref
3 CONC ormed cemetery" as opposed to Lutheran burials..
2 NOTE https://www.americanantiquarian.org/proceedings/44539283.pdf
2 NOTE AI Overview
3 CONT
3 CONT "The main difference between Reformed and Lutheran denominations lies in their understanding of Christ's presence in the Lord's Supper, with Reformed theology believing Christ is present spiritually while Lutheran theology teaches
3 CONC  a physical presence of Christ in the sacrament; this difference stems from broader theological variations regarding predestination and God's grace, where Reformed theology leans more towards Calvinist ideas of predestination and 
3 CONC Lutherans emphasize God's unconditional grace for all people."
2 NOTE Gen. Peter Muhlenberf was 1st a Lutheram, then an Episcopal minister, then a soldier, then a Senator
2 NOTE see also
3 CONT Theological Quarterly Vol. 6, No.1; Jan 1902 pages 54 et seq. [https://ctsfw.net/media/pdfs/GraebnerALTheologicalTrainingintheEarlyLutheranChurchofAmericapart1.pdf "THEOLOGICAL TRAINING IN THE EARLY LUTHERAN CHURCH OF AMERICA"]
