1 TITL count
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Casimir-Constantin Plater (1749 – 4 August 1807) – statesman of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania, castellan of Trakai (1790–1793), last vice-chancellor of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania (1793–1795), historian and publici
2 CONC st. Starost (the Elder) of Livonia and Dinaburg. Royal Chamberlain and Captain of the people's cavalry.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Biography'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Representative of the noble Plater family of the Plater coat of arms. Son of the Voivode of Mstislav and Castellan of Traka, Count Constantine-Ludwig Plater (1722–1778), and Augusta Oginska, daughter of the Voivode of Trakai, Duke
2 CONC  Josef Thaddeus Oginski.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1773, Casimir-Constantin Plater received the title of Count. In 1787, he was appointed Grand Chancellor of Lithuania but refused this position. He was Castellan of Trakai from 1790 to 1793 and the last Deputy Chancellor of the 
2 CONC Grand Chancellor of Lithuania from 1793 to 1795. He recognized the new Polish constitution adopted on May 3, 1791.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1774, he founded the ancestral commandery of the Order of Malta in the village of Kalnik in the Vitebsk region.
2 CONT
2 CONT Casimir Konstantin Plater was the author of the diary of the Polish king Stanisław August Poniatowski's journey to Kanev to meet with the Russian Empress Catherine II the Great in 1787 and to St. Petersburg in 1792.
2 CONT
2 CONT Casimir-Constantin Plater was a member of the Permanent Council from 1776 to 1788 and 1793 to 1794. He was also an active participant in the Targowica Confederation in 1792.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1793, at the Grodno Sejm, Casimir Konstanty Plater was appointed by Polish King Stanislaw August Poniatowski as a member of the Sejm delegation for negotiations with the Russian ambassador Jacob Sievers. He was a member of the 
2 CONC Grodno Confederation, created at the Sejm to approve the Second Partition of the Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth—the occupation of those countries by the Russian Empire. In 1793, he was appointed a member of the Educational Commiss
2 CONC ion of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania from the Targowica Confederation.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Member of the Order of Malta (1775),
2 CONT * Knight of the Order of St. Stanislaus (1776),
2 CONT * awarded by the Order of St. Alexander Nevsky (1787)
2 CONT * awarded by the Order of the White Eagle (1791).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Family and Children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Count Casimir Konstantin Plater married Isabella Ludwika Borch, daughter of the Chancellor of the Grand Crown Jan-Andrey von Borch. The following children were born of this union:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Ludwig
2 CONT * Michael
2 CONT * Constantine
2 CONT * Jan
2 CONT * Casimir
2 CONT * Stanislaus
2 CONT * Heinrich
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael Plater - married Isabella Helena, the last representative of the baronial family of Zyberg, and received the double surname Plater-Zyberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
