1 NOTE {geni:about_me} All primary source documents from Latvia (fund no.1100, inventory no.13, file no.1079 in Riga Historical Archive)have his name as George Fredrick Wilhelm though like his uncle George Frederick Ernst only used his f
2 CONC irst two names.  He is always indicated as the winner of Pour Le Merite.  In Struteln records, he is shown to have been in Holland (his battle point where he won the award, North America which in their parlance would include the W
2 CONC est Indies and in the final tree which was added to and corrected  as required as still in England, a note sustained after 1818 meaning that he was still living then as well.  This all perfectly meshes with his extensively known h
2 CONC istory after Latvia.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is the only one of the family noted as leaving home as a child  1777 - father dies - guardianship of Uncle Dietrich 1778-1782 schooling in Nurmhusem 1783-1785. Became  2nd lieutenant in Prussian foreign service, Sept 28, 1785 -
2 CONC 1787 at Gowerwell, Holland 1790-1791 For service in Mittenwalde and Potsdam 1790 wins Pour Le Merite for service in Gowerwell, Holland in 1787 - full account online - notation Emil v. Pfeilitzer 1790 Promoted to Captain - Emil and
2 CONC  primary documents 1791 lobbies guardian Uncle Dietrich to allow him to buy land, presumably around Potsdam with his prize money.  1793 Hanoverian Recruiter in Prussia - English source  Promoted to Major for Von Hompesch 1794-1795
2 CONC  (including his signed report from the Dons papers - British Library and Viscomte Grouvel Major for the Duke of York Rangers (1795 - Holland Prussia (including his signed report from the Dons papers British Library and Viscomte Gr
2 CONC ouvel which explains how he would have got to St Kitts), 1796-1797 - Dominican Republic, Tobago and  St Kitts 1797 Married to Anna-Maduit Garvey, Sister of Captain John Garvey, St George, Basseterre, St Kitts Lena Boyd Brown, Vere
2 CONC  Langford Oliver 1797 - becomes Colonel 1798 - daughter Anne born in England  1799-  daughter Marie born in England - birth certificate  1799 - end of military career - settles in London in a civil service capacity - still Russian
2 CONC  via Courland.   1802 - Portrait of The Children of Baron Pfeilitzer - 3 children playing on a rocking chair - Jean-Jacques Masquerier for the Royal Academy - 3rd child, boy?, older? different mother - something in 1792?  1802, br
2 CONC other Captain Jack Garvey assassinated 1802 in St Kitts (Burke's foreign events) 1806 Anna dies and two girls sent to the last sibling left (Jasper Maudit Garvey had predeceased, John and Anna, so the honour falls to Lucas in Lond
2 CONC on).  The third child - no record of birth or death in England - to George's family?  He is always on the road it seems. 1807  - George marries the widow of Lieutenant- Colonel James Inness 1810 - intentionally holds a party with 
2 CONC several very interesting Hanoverian folks in Bath, including mistresses, with the title "Fashionable Intelligence" and called a "déjeuné a L'ambigu"  Was this an intelligence operation.  This terms does not appear in the French di
2 CONC ctionary until the 1830's - Seems he invented this term which caught on!!!   1818 intentionally recorded visitor along with the royal chaplain to Frederick VI, Landgrave of Hesse-Homburg just before talks of the marriage to Prince
2 CONC ss Elizabeth of the United Kingdom begin 1819-1832 off and on in Tobago.   During leave, he is a regular attendee of the Prince's Levee.   To this point, it looks like the Pfeiltizer, Garvey, Mauduit, Inness and other family relat
2 CONC ed interests were on the verge of further expansion of trade routes of unprecedented proportons  but this ends up being the end of the ultimate plan
2 CONT
2 CONT In retrospect it does appear that George's addition to the family to the Mauduit-Garvey was intended to connect Eastern and Western Europe to their existing trade routes centred on the cloth industry of Mauduit, Wright and Company
2 CONC  (which would originally been Mauduit, Garvey and Company had uncles Jasper Mauduit Garvey not passed as away a child) from St. Kitts and throughout the Caribbean, to Maryland (the First Families of Prince George's County) and New
2 CONC foundland under the jurisdiction of the colonial agents, Uncle Jasper and Israel Mauduit, connecting to London to Uncle William Mauduit and Lucas Garvey stationed there.  George's close connection with the Hanoverian Princes and t
2 CONC he residue from the Germanophile Russian groups from von Biron onward and enabled by the many languages and countries with whom George had relationships.
2 CONT
2 CONT Unfortunately, even with the all of the best laid, plans, unfortunate deaths creating family pressures, the loss of over 30,000 pounds in a massive fraudulent bank (perpetrator was executed) which impacted many wealthy Londoners a
2 CONC nd the gross mismanagement of Governor Frederick Philipse Robinson who the entire Tobaggan assembly tried to impeach with over 100 charges) pushed these plans into a tailspin
2 CONT
2 CONT 1819-1837 West Indies recap
2 CONT
2 CONT 1819 - 1829 - Collector of Customs (like the head of a tax service for Tobago
2 CONT 1830 - 1832 - Adds the role of Comptroller, the most powerful financial official for Tobago
2 CONT 1832 - keeps both roles for Tobago and adds both roles also for Barbados
2 CONT
2 CONT 1840-1853 - according to census reports, he and his step son in law, Sir Richard Drakeford Hennegan live nearby to each other on Great Russel Street,  A full account is found in the Diaries of Edmund Yates.  At this time George an
2 CONC d Lord Edmund Byng are very good friends and George seems to be the "Lord" of nearby Clarges Street not far from the palace where many dignitaries could be found.  He enjoys a newfound life of a well pensioned retiree, again well 
2 CONC ahead of his time.
